{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/1432569-harvester-question-when-making-the-kill-counts-as-making-the-kill","id":1432569,"title":"Harvester Question: When making the kill counts as making the kill","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-01-17T18:57:26-08:00","id":1432569,"title":"Harvester Question: When making the kill counts as making the kill","content":"A brief, weird question. For those who don't have WOD: Immortals, a Harvester is a kind of parasitic immortal that maintains itself by hunting and killing other immortals. They use a ritualistic tool that essentially &quot;steals&quot; the immortality from their victim- every century of life grants three years of immortality to the Harvester.<br /> <br /> So then we come to Mummies. Pretty sweet if you can take one down- 6000 years old equals a cool 180 years of immortality for the Harvester. Except....Mummies have a nasty have of shrugging off being murdered. So would you consider a Harvester that kills a mummy successful even though the Arisen is one resurrection roll away from popping back up?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-18T14:29:52-08:00","id":1432630,"title":"","content":"Between the Mummy's ability to &quot;not get murdered&quot; and the Harvester's ability to &quot;steal another's life&quot;, all I can say is &quot;why not both?&quot;<br /> <br /> I mean, it is objectively true that if a Harvester kills an immortal being (which, within Mummy's system, probably means they have Sekhem score), they can still their life. Mummies, as a Sekhem powered entities, are immortal beings. As such, the Harvesters can and should be able to Harvest a Mummy for Sekhem. However, Mummies also have a strange ability to defy causality due to their existence in a non linear timelines. As such, they can ignore temporal paradoxes, such as being killed by an Harvester. In that case, you have a number of potential ways in order to deal with it-<br /> <br /> 1) Nothing. The Harvester immortality method is based around other individuals which hold the Endless Merit. Mummies don't have the Endless Merit and as such give no benefit to the Harvester (boring, yet a solid solution).<br /> <br /> 2) Modify the Harvester immortality method to work on Sekehm instead of lifetime. The Harvester gains lifespan depends on the Sekhem score of what they kill, making mummies an attractive, even if dangerous target.<br /> <br /> 3) While the Mummy experience time non linearly, the Harvester experience time linearly. When the Harvester kills a mummy, they gain an amount of life equal to the linear timeline in which they live. The mummy, however, can be reborn later as normal, which means that the Harvester could in theory kill the same mummy again and again and increase their lifespan... or create a very deadly enemy which would haunt them in the future.<br /> <br /> 4) Same as (3), with a slight change- when an Harvester kills a mummy, they <i>kill the mummy</i><i>. </i>The Harvester method is a foul ceremony, an antithesis for the Rite of Return. Where the Rite makes sure nothing will end, the Harvester's Scythe guarantees that <i>things have met their end</i>. A mummy killed by the Scythe is forever destroyed.... even though you may rule that by doing so, the Scythe is destroyed as well. You could still give the Harvester the lifespan (they killed the mummy, they earned it!), even though they can't replenish it by any means (beside stealing another Scythe, but you could also state that no Scythe or other immortality method would work for them after doing such a terrible act), or that you could say that by having the Scythe destroyed they lose immortality all together, making it an heroic sacrifice. If you are exceptionally cruel, you say that the act would kill the Harvester together with the mummy, but I'm not sure it makes sense.<br /> <br /> 5) the Mummy is a non linear entity, which lives since the creation of the world to its eventual end at the belly of Ammut. When an Harvester kills the Mummy, they try to Harvest infinity. Multiplying infinity by zero is not defined, and the act could shatter time itself. Perhaps the Harvester becomes the new Mummy, taking the status from the old one and sends the ancient soul screaming to the Underworld as they go through the Trials of Duat to become servant of the Judges. Maybe the Arisen's tendency to remove paradoxes results with them <i>becoming the Harvester</i>, living their whole life as the person who once killed and absorbing their traits and personality into their soul, as the Harvester discovers they tried to bite on more they could chew. Maybe the result will be a clean, unbound immortal, free from Judgement and reach Apotheosis of some sort, yet still haunted by the Judges and their spawn. Maybe they become an amalgamation of the two entities, trying to reconcile the two lifelines which became one. Maybe they instead became one of the Wadget Itja, which is their closest parallel while the Mummy returns to life. Maybe it will just end with a large explosion which will destroy anything in a range of kilometers, leaving only the Harvester and the Mummy standing. Maybe it would break time, summon Ammut, Fate and/or the Judges and ends with total chaos for centuries.<br /> <br /> In short, it is an interesting scenario which could go in many different ways, depending on how you want to play it and the role that the Arisen and Harvester of interest take in the Chronicle (character origin story, protagonist, antagonist, background character, plot hook, etc)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","user":"LostLight","date":". 01-18-2021, 05:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}